Figure  11  shows the variation of the melt pool length and aspect ratio with respect to substrate initial temperature at a given time, laser power, and scanning speed. The length and aspect ratios increase with increasing substrate initial temperature. However, compared to the cases of the laser power and scanning speed variation, the length and aspect ratio are least sensitive to the variation of the substrate’s initial temperature. This lack of sensitivity to the initial temperature of the substrate can be understood from a comparison of the thermal energy supplied by laser and the thermal energy needed to raise the substrate initial temperature. During the fabrication process, powder needs to absorb sufficient thermal energy to raise its temperature from the initial substrate temperature to the liquidus temperature (1933 K) and to melt (enthalpy of melting). Laser power and scanning speed control the supply of this thermal energy. It is considerably larger than the thermal energy supplied to the substrate in advance by raising its temperature to values up to 460 K from 300 K. Consequently, by raising the initial temperature of the substrate, we do not change the melt pool formation process substantially. Consequently, compared to laser power and scanning speed, the initial substrate temperature does not have a strong effect on the length and aspect ratio of the melt pool. Note that the maximum error in the length and aspect ratio prediction is  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
